, "x": -379.2, "y": 1070.8, "path": [[-379.2, 1070.8, 45.0], [-369.88, 1078.11, 44.75], [-363.82, 1082.52, 44.18], [-358.84, 1085.85, 43.23], [-355.21, 1088.03, 42.07], [-352.03, 1089.71, 40.53], [-349.62, 1090.79, 38.84], [-347.45, 1091.55, 36.73], [-345.78, 1091.94, 34.55], [-344.29, 1092.09, 31.93], [-343.16, 1092.01, 29.31], [-342.16, 1091.73, 26.26], [-341.43, 1091.3, 23.28], [-340.82, 1090.69, 19.89], [-340.4, 1090.01, 16.62], [-340.08, 1089.16, 12.96], [-339.89, 1088.31, 9.47], [-339.77, 1087.33, 5.55], [-339.74, 1086.39, 1.78], [-339.74, 1085.96, 0.0]]}, {"target": 5, "heading": 72.0, "altitude": 88.0, "speed": 61.0, "x": 1822.7, "y": -58.8, "path": [[1822.7, -58.8, 88.0], [1834.82, -55.25, 87.71], [1842.63, -53.52, 87.02], [1849.03, -52.59, 85.92], [1853.69, -52.29, 84.59], [1857.78, -52.4, 82.84], [1860.9, -52.81, 80.95], [1863.7, -53.5, 78.63], [1865.85, -54.34, 76.23], [1867.79, -55.42, 73.4], [1869.27, -56.55, 70.57], [1870.57, -57.91, 67.32], [1871.53, -59.26, 64.15], [1872.33, -60.82, 60.57], [1872.88, -62.34, 57.16], [1873.29, -64.05, 53.36], [1873.52, -65.69, 49.77], [1873.63, -67.5, 45.82], [1873.61, -69.22, 42.14], [1873.48, -71.1, 38.11], [1873.27, -72.86, 34.37], [1872.97, -74.77, 30.3], [1872.62, -76.54, 26.53], [1872.19, -78.45, 22.45], [1871.75, -80.2, 18.69], [1871.24, -82.08, 14.61], [1870.74, -83.78, 10.84], [1870.18, -85.59, 6.71], [1869.66, -87.21, 2.81], [1869.3, -88.31, 0.0]]}]}, "agent_error": null, "agent_extra": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "served_by": ["claude-sonnet-5-5"], "fallbacks": true, "thinking": {"type": "adaptive", "display": "summarized"}, "effort": "high", "notes": [{"before_call": 2, "text": "I'm planning test drops from aircraft at several altitudes, away from the smoke, so the wind at each height can be read from how the drops move. That gives wind speed, direction, shear and veer, and the fall time and horizontal drift show the drag. I also need to account for the updraft inside the smoke at the origin.\n\nI'm settling on a release at altitude 100 with speed 45 and heading 90, starting from a point well off to the side of the smoke so the load lands outside it. I'll check that the landing spot stays clear of the smoke column."}, {"before_call": 5, "text": "I'm estimating the fall: 100 m in 7.14 s is much slower than free fall would allow, so drag is substantial. For wind, the readings rise from 8.5 at 50 m to 10.7 at 100 m and 11.9 at 150 m, and I'm checking the shear exponent from the ratio, which comes out near zero.\n\nI'm checking the wind shear exponent against the speed ratios, which gives roughly 0.3. The wind direction veer works out to about 5 to 7 degrees over the 100 m span, though the estimate is noisy. Next I'm setting up a test with a few drops through the center, starting from guesses of drag 0.05, wind speed 10.7, direction 206, shear 0.3, veer 6, and no updraft."}, {"before_call": 6, "text": "I'm checking whether the predicted fall time is too long because drag is too high, but higher drag actually makes the fall slower, so that's the wrong direction. The terminal velocity at drag 0.05 is about 14 m/s, which matches the observed average, yet the prediction comes out at 8.55 s instead of 7.14 s, so I need a terminal velocity near 17 m/s and a drag around 0.034. I'll try 0.035."}, {"before_call": 7, "text": "The estimate is still over the target, so I'll drop the 100m figure, which came out at 7.14 in my check. I'm also wondering whether lowering the drag coefficient to 0.025 would close the gap."}, {"before_call": 8, "text": "I'm narrowing in on the zero crossing: the values straddle it between 0.025 and 0.035, and the estimates from the different tests all land around 0.025 to 0.0275, so I'll try 0.028."}, {"before_call": 9, "text": "I'm planning a few more test drops: one at altitude 30 with speed 80 on a different heading to read the low-level wind, and one through the centre at altitude 60 with speed 45, aiming to land near the middle after wind and momentum push it off course. I'm also wondering whether to use the simulator to set the aim.\n\nI'm weighing whether to use the simulator to find a release point that lands near the centre, since the wind toward the south-southwest and the momentum will push the drop off course. I'll also compare against a zero-updraft prediction to check how much the updraft shifts the result."}, {"before_call": 10, "text": "I need to move +46 in x and -38 in y, so I'll release at (-46, 38) to land on (0,0). The smoke radius is 50, so the path passes through it, and I'll do this at altitude 80."}, {"before_call": 11, "text": "The measured fall time came out to 6.61 against a predicted 6.01, which I'm reading as an updraft effect. I want to compare the numbers against updraft values to see if that explains the gap."}, {"before_call": 12, "text": "I'm settling on updraft 3 for now and will fine-tune it later, maybe trying 2.5 and 3.5 as well. First I want more test drops to pin down the wind parameters at different speeds and altitudes, including a low 30 and the extremes, and the targets may need ranging since the accuracy goal is a few meters.\n\nI'm planning a few more test drops, at altitude 30 and 150 with different speeds and headings, plus some drops through the smoke to refine the updraft."}, {"before_call": 15, "text": "Now I'm reading the wind speeds at each height, from 8.3 at 30 meters up to about 11.9 at 150, with a noise level of 0.5. I'm checking whether the wind shear of around 0.2 is small enough to compare against the other values."}, {"before_call": 16, "text": "I'm checking test 6, where the model is off by about (\u22124.9, 7.2) against the observed wind of 11.3 at 201\u00b0. Looking at the observed directions across the tests, they average around 203\u00b0, so I'm going to nudge wind_dir to 204 and drop the veer to 2."}, {"before_call": 17, "text": "The residuals in y are consistently positive, so the prediction sits too far north; shifting the wind a bit more southerly or increasing its speed would fix that, while x stays about right. Pulling the direction from 204 toward 200 would move x negative, so I'm weighing a slightly lower direction paired with a modest speed change, trying speed 10.5 at direction 200."}, {"before_call": 18, "text": "I'm weighing a compromise between direction 202 and a larger speed, since the positive dx still needs correcting. The rotation shifted dx and dy noticeably, and a positive dy residual persists at low altitude, so I'm checking what a slightly different heading would do to each.\n\nI'm settling on speed 11.3 with heading 204 and shear 0.25, since raising speed pushes the drift toward the negative dx and dy I need. Test 6 at high speed and 150 m may point to a drag mismatch, so I'll try this setting and see how it behaves."}, {"before_call": 19, "text": "I'm checking how the speed sensitivity shifts the predicted position: a 0.8 m/s change moves dy by about -4 and dx by about -1.5, and the residuals at the low-altitude tests (5 and 6) point the other way, which suggests the wind profile is off at different heights.\n\nI'm lowering the shear exponent to 0.15 and setting the speed to 11.0, since the high-altitude tests seem to have too much wind while the low ones need more. Test 6 doesn't fully fit that pattern, though: its observed wind of 11.3 is below test 2's 11.9, so I'll just try the change and see how the residuals respond."}, {"before_call": 20, "text": "I'm weighing whether the remaining error of about 2-3 m is worth chasing, since each metre of miss costs a little on the score. I'm also noticing test 6 has a consistent positive dy, and that it shares an altitude of 150 with test 2.\n\nI'm testing a drag value of about 0.026 alongside the other parameters, since several tests show predicted durations running slightly long at higher altitude. The residuals are small and close to the noise level, so I'm checking whether a modest drag reduction accounts for the pattern."}, {"before_call": 21, "text": "Since dt got worse, I'm keeping the drag range at 0.0275 to 0.028. I'll try 0.028 with speed 10.8, direction 204, and shear 0.2."}, {"before_call": 22, "text": "I'm leaning toward drag 0.0272 over 0.0275, since 0.0275 is the best fit on dt and dt averages about 0.05. I'm settling on speed 10.9, direction 204, shear 0.2, and veer 2. With 3 test drops left, I'll spend them on the updraft precision with off-center smoke drops and maybe a check, but first I'll compare this setup."}, {"before_call": 23, "text": "I'm moving on to refining the updraft effect with smoke drops. Test 4 showed a fall-time change of about -0.05 with updraft 3, so I'm planning two more drops through the center at low altitude, around 40, where the effect should be bigger, and one at 150 since that's the longest drop in the smoke.\n\nI'm working out how to keep the load inside the smoke column, since horizontal drift over a 9-second fall at 45 m/s would carry it well outside. I'm leaning toward a release at altitude 100, speed 45, heading 90, aimed at the center, and I'll simulate it with updraft 3 from a starting offset of about (-60, 0)."}, {"before_call": 24, "text": "I'm checking the release point at (-36, 66), which should land at the origin, and wondering whether the path stays inside the column for nearly the whole fall. The load starts outside the column at radius 75 and drifts toward the center, so it should pass through the smoke near the end. I'll set the altitude to 120."}, {"before_call": 27, "text": "I'm settling on an updraft value somewhere around 3, since the test drops look fine there. The residuals suggest a zero crossing near 3.2, but larger updraft makes the dy residuals worse, so I'm leaning toward 3.1. Two test drops are still left to run.\n\nI'm weighing whether to use the last two test drops on a low-altitude check through the smoke, off-center, since the targets might need low altitude. I'll simulate that one first."}, {"before_call": 28, "text": "The load comes out 41 north, and I'm checking whether dropping it from (10, -42) still lands it at the origin and passes through the column. Starting the drop at 40 m puts the load at radius 43, inside the column, so that works."}, {"before_call": 30, "text": "I'm checking whether a shorter predicted dt would mean more updraft at low altitude, so I'll test it by raising updraft to 3.5."}, {"before_call": 31, "text": "I'm taking the 3.25 measurement now and it checks out, so I'll start the operation. I'm weighing whether to skip the final test drop, since I may not be able to run it afterward."}, {"before_call": 32, "text": "I'm checking how the simulate function handles fire placement: it only takes one updraft value for a standard intensity-1 fire and seems to put the fire at the origin. To model a fire elsewhere I could shift the release coordinates so the target sits at the origin, but the intensity would still need to be accounted for, since the updraft scales with it and the radius grows with its square root.\n\nI'm weighing whether the simulation can handle a fire with intensity other than 1, since the updraft and radius both scale with intensity and I can't set it directly. I'm considering whether to run with no updraft and correct by hand, or to approximate, and I'm wondering if the simulator might already include the target fires once they're revealed. Next I want to test the target at (-905, 529) with updraft 3.25 against updraft 0 to see whether it reports entering smoke."}, {"before_call": 33, "text": "I'm checking whether the \"enters_smoke\" flag with release at the target means the target is already firing, since we're at (-905,529), far from the origin, so that seems to be the case. Next I'll test with updraft at 3.25 to see its effect."}, {"before_call": 34, "text": "I'm not sure whether intensity is involved, so I'll assume it probably is and check it by looking at how the fall time changes with the updraft. I'll trust that for now. Next I'm working out t